You are tasked with implementing a method to track the time taken by a specific operation within a Java program. The given code snippet is part of a class `WCPEngine` which is used for performance analysis. The class contains a `HashMap` named `lockToThreadSet` that maps a lock identifier to a set of thread identifiers. Additionally, it initializes a `HashSet` named `threadSet`, and a `WCPEvent` named `handlerEvent`. The code snippet also sets a boolean variable `time_reporting` to `true` and initializes a `startTimeAnalysis` variable to track the start time of the analysis.

Your task is to implement a method `trackOperationTime` within the `WCPEngine` class that takes the name of the operation as a parameter and returns the time taken to execute that operation in milliseconds. The method should start tracking time when it is called and stop tracking time when the operation is completed. You should use the `System.currentTimeMillis()` method to capture the start and end times of the operation.

Your implementation should handle cases where the same operation is tracked multiple times and should return the cumulative time taken for all invocations of the operation.

```java
public class WCPEngine {
    private HashMap<String, HashSet<String>> lockToThreadSet;
    private HashSet<Thread> threadSet;
    private WCPEvent handlerEvent;
    private boolean time_reporting = true;
    private long startTimeAnalysis;

    public WCPEngine(ParserType pType, String trace_folder) {
        super(pType);
        this.threadSet = new HashSet<Thread>();
        initializeReader(trace_folder);
        handlerEvent = new WCPEvent();
        startTimeAnalysis = time_reporting ? System.currentTimeMillis() : 0;
    }

    public long trackOperationTime(String operationName) {
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        // Perform the operation here
        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        return endTime - startTime;
    }
}
```

